// @check
// @error cannot assign null to i32
// CONTRACT (must-error): THE SOUNDNESS BOUNDARY OF THE LOOP-BODY NARROWING.
//
// A `while e != null { … }` body reads `e` narrowed, and this file pins the price: inside
// that body the narrowed type is the binding's ACCEPT contract too, so writing a null
// back is a type error. Nothing invalidates the fact at the assignment and nothing needs
// to — the write that would falsify it cannot be spelled.
//
// This is not a rule invented for loops. The `if` arm has always behaved this way (`if e
// != null { e = null }` is the same diagnostic at the same column), and matching it
// verbatim is what makes a loop body sound with no flow analysis: a literal `null`, and a
// nullable call's result (`cannot assign i32? to i32`), are both rejected, while a
// non-null write keeps the fact true — the accepted half is `MUT-i32` in
// `while-body-narrowing.vl`.
//
// The pre-fix behavior was the reverse and is what makes this worth pinning: the body was
// not a narrowing scope, so `e = null` was legal there and `e + 1` was the error instead.
// A future change that widens the narrowing without carrying the write contract with it
// turns this file green.
function f(e: i32 | null) {
  while e != null {
    e = null
    return e + 1
  }
  return 0
}
print(toString(f(5)))
